The defect is in the `combineCorners` branch of the new `gsBoxTopology::allComponents`:

```cpp
else
{
    component_coll_t last;
    for( typename map_t::iterator it = comps[i].begin(); it != comps[i].end(); ++it )
    {
        const index_t nrcp = it->second.size();
        for (index_t j=0; j<nrcp; ++j)
            last.push_back(it->second[i]);
    }
}
```

1. **`last` is never added to `result`.** It is built and then goes out of scope, so `allComponents(true)` returns no corner components at all.
2. **Wrong index in the inner loop.** It uses `it->second[i]` instead of `it->second[j]`. In this branch `i == 0`, so the first `patchComponent` of each group is pushed `nrcp` times, and the other patch-corner representations are lost.

**Why it matters at runtime:** the new example calls `mb.topology().allComponents(true)` in `setupSubspaceCorrectedMassSmoother`. Edges and faces are fetched with `noBoundary = true`, which strips their corner dofs. Because the combined corner component is missing, the corner dofs are covered by no transfer matrix. The additive smoother therefore never updates those degrees of freedom.

The fix is to use `[j]` and to push `last` into `result` after the loop, guarding against an empty `last`.

The new `gsMultiPatch::closestPointTo` has a data race whenever OpenMP is enabled:

```cpp
gsVector<T> tmp;
...
#   pragma omp parallel for default(shared) reduction(minimum:cph)
for (size_t k = 0; k!= m_patches.size(); ++k)
{
    T val = this->patch(k).closestPointTo(pt, tmp, accuracy);
    if (val<cph.dist)
    {
        ...
        cph.preim = tmp;
    }
}
```

- **Shared output buffer:** `tmp` is declared outside the parallel region, and `default(shared)` makes it shared by all threads. It is not listed as `private(tmp)`.
- **What goes wrong:** Each iteration passes `tmp` to `patch(k).closestPointTo(pt, tmp, ...)`, which resizes and writes it, then copies it into `cph.preim`. Several threads therefore write the same vector concurrently.
- **Consequences:** The result is undefined behaviour. It can crash or corrupt the heap during the concurrent resize. It can also return a `preim` computed on a different patch than `cph.pid`, pairing a patch index with the wrong parameters.
- **Fix:** Add `private(tmp)` to the pragma, or declare `tmp` inside the loop body.

A second problem is in the same added lines. The `declare reduction` names the type `struct closestPointHelper`, but the struct is called `__closestPointHelper`. So `reduction(minimum:cph)` has no matching user-defined reduction for `cph`'s type, which is a compile error in OpenMP builds.

Both defects are in lines this commit adds.
